"""Paper-style topics and a closest-country reader; no abstract similarity map."""
import json,html,base64
PAPER='https://wp.lancs.ac.uk/ffmm2024/files/2024/09/FFMM-2024-018-An-Analysis-of-Market-Manipulation-Definitions.pdf'
def panel(O):
 r=json.loads((O/'full250/analysis/similarity_breakdown.json').read_text('utf8'));esc=lambda x:html.escape(str(x))
 image='data:image/png;base64,'+base64.b64encode((O/'full250/figures/word_frequency_ranking.png').read_bytes()).decode()
 N={c['iso3']:c['country'] for c in r['countries']}
 topics=''.join('<tr><td><b>Theme '+str(t['topic'])+'</b></td><td>'+esc(', '.join(t['keywords']))+'</td><td><b>'+f'{t["percent"]:.1f}%</b><br><small>{t["count"]} texts</small></td><td><details><summary>Show countries</summary>'+esc('; '.join(N[i]+' ('+i+')' for i in t['countries']))+'</details></td></tr>' for t in r['topics'])
 checks=''.join('<tr><td>'+esc(x['sample'])+'</td><td>'+str(x['n'])+'</td><td>'+f'{x["pairwise_similarity_correlation"]:.3f}'+'</td></tr>' for x in r['sensitivities'])
 readable=''.join('<tr><td>'+esc(x['sample'])+'</td><td>'+str(x['n'])+'</td>'+''.join('<td>'+f'{x[k]:.1f}'+'</td>' for k in ['words_per_sentence','dale_chall','gunning_fog','flesch_kincaid'])+'</tr>' for x in r['readability_summary'])
 markup=f'''<p>Compare <b>{r['sample_n']} country texts</b>: {r['official_english_n']} official English sources and {r['checked_translation_n']} checked translations, treated equally as English analysis texts.</p>
<h3>Break down a country’s closest matches</h3><label for="similarityCountry">Choose a country</label><select id="similarityCountry"></select><p id="similarityBasis" class="small"></p><div id="similarityMatches"></div>
<p class="small">Similarity runs from 0 to 100 and measures overlap in weighted vocabulary. The table shows the five closest texts and the words contributing most to each match. Translation and short-passage labels help you inspect the underlying material. Click a country name to read its wording.</p>
<h3>Recurring wording themes</h3><p>Following the financial-manipulation paper’s topic-table approach, the model groups recurring words into {r['selected_topics']} themes. Each text is counted under its strongest theme; a text can contain language from several themes.</p><table id="wordingTopicTable"><thead><tr><th>Theme</th><th>Words that define it</th><th>Share of texts</th><th>Countries</th></tr></thead><tbody>{topics}</tbody></table>
<details class="context-details"><summary>Which words appear most often?</summary><figure><img src="{image}" alt="Ranked bars of words used in selected English and translated texts"><figcaption>Percentage of selected texts containing each word after combining grammatical forms and removing country names and common boilerplate. A word’s presence alone does not establish a legal requirement.</figcaption></figure></details>
<details class="context-details"><summary>Other ways to present these comparisons</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> keep the closest-matches tool and keyword table above. They connect each comparison directly to countries and words.</p><p><b>Country theme profiles:</b> stacked bars could show how much of each country’s wording belongs to each theme. Useful for comparing a small selected group.</p><p><b>Explicit legal conditions:</b> a country table with ticks for reports, product sameness and local review would describe legal content more directly. It would use the reviewed condition coding rather than vocabulary similarity.</p></details>
<details class="context-details"><summary>Method, translations and the financial-manipulation paper</summary><p><a href="{PAPER}">Gurrola-Pérez and Lin (2024), Section 5</a> present important words, a two-axis plot of <b>words</b>, topic tables listing keywords and jurisdiction counts, and readability tables. Their Figure 6 plots words; the earlier chart on this page plotted countries. We adopt their topic-table presentation and part-of-speech-aware lemmatisation, while adding the closest-country reader.</p><p>We use nouns, verbs, adjectives and adverbs, retain legal modals and negation, combine word forms using WordNet, and remove country/agency names and repeated boilerplate. A fixed random 75% sample selects the number of LDA topics from 2–5 using UMass word coherence; the chosen model is then fitted on all {r['sample_n']} texts. Labels above list the actual highest-weight words. This is an adaptation of the paper, with the coherence measure and seed checks stated explicitly.</p><p><b>Translation treatment:</b> all {r['checked_translation_n']} checked primary-document translations enter the main model with the same document weight as official English texts. The source law remains available in its original language. Unlike the paper’s official translations, these are labelled checked research translations. Unchecked or incomplete translations are retained for further review. There are {r['short_excerpt_n']} passages shorter than 80 English words; they remain in the main comparison and are flagged.</p><p>The model uses {r['primary_n']} selected national pathway texts and {r['sample_n']-r['primary_n']} narrower or explanatory sources. These text-model counts differ from the 89-pathway legal-condition sample.</p><p>Across ten alternative random seeds, median agreement in the strongest-theme assignment is {r['topic_median_ari']:.2f} on the adjusted Rand index. Themes are exploratory descriptions of wording, rather than definitive legal classes.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Sensitivity sample</th><th>Texts</th><th>Similarity correlation with main model</th></tr></thead><tbody>{checks}</tbody></table><p>The former components 1 and 2 were mathematical weighted word patterns. They explained 12.1% of variation in the former 48-text sample. The current closest-match scores use the full vocabulary.</p><h3>Readability, following the paper’s Table 3</h3><table><thead><tr><th>English analysis text</th><th>Texts</th><th>Words per sentence</th><th>Dale–Chall</th><th>Gunning Fog</th><th>Flesch–Kincaid</th></tr></thead><tbody>{readable}</tbody></table><p>Entries are medians of formula scores. Translated scores describe the English rendering, not original-language legal complexity. Sentence splitting and excerpt length affect these diagnostics; the formula scores are not literal years of education or compliance costs.</p></details>'''
 js='const SIM='+json.dumps(r,ensure_ascii=False).replace('</','<\\/')+r''';
const simCountry=document.getElementById('similarityCountry');simCountry.innerHTML=SIM.countries.slice().sort((a,b)=>a.country.localeCompare(b.country)).map(c=>'<option value="'+esc(c.iso3)+'">'+esc(c.country)+' ('+esc(c.iso3)+')'+(c.checked_translation?' · translated':'')+'</option>').join('');simCountry.value='GBR';
function renderSimilarity(){const iso=simCountry.value,country=SIM.countries.find(c=>c.iso3===iso);document.getElementById('similarityBasis').textContent=(country.checked_translation?'Checked translation':'Official English source')+' · '+country.words+' English words'+(country.short_excerpt?' · short passage':'');document.getElementById('similarityMatches').innerHTML='<table><thead><tr><th>Closest country text</th><th>Similarity, 0–100</th><th>Words contributing most to the match</th></tr></thead><tbody>'+SIM.neighbors[iso].slice(0,5).map(c=>'<tr><td><button type="button" class="similarity-wording-link" data-iso="'+esc(c.iso3)+'">'+esc(c.country)+' ('+esc(c.iso3)+')</button><br><small>'+(c.checked_translation?'Checked translation':'Official English')+(c.short_excerpt?' · short passage':'')+'</small></td><td><b>'+c.score.toFixed(1)+'</b><div style="height:7px;background:#dce8df;max-width:150px"><div style="height:7px;background:#176b64;width:'+c.score+'%"></div></div></td><td>'+esc(c.shared_words.join(', '))+'</td></tr>').join('')+'</tbody></table>';document.querySelectorAll('.similarity-wording-link').forEach(b=>b.addEventListener('click',()=>{window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('reliance-country',{detail:{iso3:b.dataset.iso}}));document.getElementById('wording').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'});}));}
simCountry.addEventListener('change',renderSimilarity);renderSimilarity();
'''
 return markup,js
